{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "dbdf32c1-18cb-46cc-b830-7fce88591072",
  "title": "Sans-IO: a network handshake with the network removed",
  "created_at": "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-07-10T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "shared_source": "// ── The protocol: a sans-IO STUN-style binding handshake ─────────────────────\n//\n// Zero dependencies. No sockets. No clock. No `Instant::now()` anywhere below.\n// You feed the machine packets and timestamps; it tells you what to send and\n// when to wake it. These same bytes run under tokio, in a no_std firmware loop,\n// and in the browser wasm sandbox that is rendering this notebook.\n\n/// Milliseconds on some clock the caller owns. Always injected, never sampled.\npub type Millis = u64;\n\n/// A datagram, as our toy protocol sees it.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Packet {\n    /// Binding request, carrying a transaction id.\n    Request { txid: u32 },\n    /// Binding success, echoing the txid and the port the server saw.\n    Response { txid: u32, mapped_port: u16 },\n}\n\n/// Where the handshake is right now.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum State {\n    /// Constructed; nothing sent yet.\n    Idle,\n    /// In flight. `attempt` is 1-based; `deadline` is when to retransmit.\n    Waiting { attempt: u32, deadline: Millis },\n    /// The server answered; we learned our reflexive port.\n    Bound { mapped_port: u16 },\n    /// Retransmit budget exhausted.\n    Failed,\n}\n\n/// The entire handshake: four methods, one `match`, a `Vec` for the outbox.\npub struct StunBinding {\n    txid: u32,\n    state: State,\n    outbox: Vec<Packet>,\n    rto: Millis,\n    max_attempts: u32,\n}\n\nimpl StunBinding {\n    /// Begin a transaction. Queues the first request immediately.\n    pub fn new(txid: u32, now: Millis, rto: Millis, max_attempts: u32) -> Self {\n        let mut binding = Self {\n            txid,\n            state: State::Idle,\n            outbox: Vec::new(),\n            rto,\n            max_attempts,\n        };\n        binding.arm(now, 1);\n        binding\n    }\n\n    /// Queue a request and set the next retransmit deadline.\n    fn arm(&mut self, now: Millis, attempt: u32) {\n        self.outbox.push(Packet::Request { txid: self.txid });\n        // RFC 5389 §7.2.1: the retransmit timeout doubles on each attempt.\n        let backoff = self.rto.saturating_mul(1u64 << (attempt - 1).min(6));\n        self.state = State::Waiting {\n            attempt,\n            deadline: now.saturating_add(backoff),\n        };\n    }\n\n    /// Feed in a received datagram.\n    pub fn handle_input(&mut self, packet: Packet, _now: Millis) {\n        if let (\n            State::Waiting { .. },\n            Packet::Response {\n                txid,\n                mapped_port,\n            },\n        ) = (self.state, packet)\n        {\n            if txid == self.txid {\n                self.state = State::Bound { mapped_port };\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Advance timers. On a missed deadline, retransmit or give up.\n    pub fn handle_timeout(&mut self, now: Millis) {\n        if let State::Waiting { attempt, deadline } = self.state {\n            if now >= deadline {\n                if attempt >= self.max_attempts {\n                    self.state = State::Failed;\n                } else {\n                    self.arm(now, attempt + 1);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Pull the next datagram the machine wants to put on the wire.\n    pub fn poll_transmit(&mut self) -> Option<Packet> {\n        if self.outbox.is_empty() {\n            None\n        } else {\n            Some(self.outbox.remove(0))\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// When should the caller next call `handle_timeout`? `None` means \"never\".\n    pub fn poll_timeout(&self) -> Option<Millis> {\n        match self.state {\n            State::Waiting { deadline, .. } => Some(deadline),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// The current state, for rendering and tests.\n    pub fn state(&self) -> State {\n        self.state\n    }\n}\n\n// ── A deterministic toy network (still zero I/O) ─────────────────────────────\n\n/// A tiny linear-congruential PRNG, so packet loss is reproducible without a\n/// `rand` dependency. Same seed, same coin flips, every single time.\npub struct Lcg(pub u64);\n\nimpl Lcg {\n    /// Advance the state and return the high bits.\n    pub fn next_u32(&mut self) -> u32 {\n        self.0 = self\n            .0\n            .wrapping_mul(6_364_136_223_846_793_005)\n            .wrapping_add(1_442_695_040_888_963_407);\n        (self.0 >> 33) as u32\n    }\n\n    /// Returns `true` with probability `loss_percent` / 100.\n    pub fn drops(&mut self, loss_percent: u32) -> bool {\n        self.next_u32() % 100 < loss_percent\n    }\n}\n\n// ── Driving the machine against that network ─────────────────────────────────\n\n/// What happened on one step of a driven run, captured for the log table.\n#[derive(Clone)]\npub struct LogRow {\n    pub clock: Millis,\n    pub kind: EventKind,\n    pub detail: String,\n    pub state: String,\n}\n\n/// The flavor of a log row, used only to color its badge.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum EventKind {\n    Send,\n    Recv,\n    Drop,\n    Timer,\n    Fail,\n}\n\n/// A short, readable name for a state (the attempt count folded in).\npub fn state_label(s: State) -> String {\n    match s {\n        State::Idle => \"Idle\".to_string(),\n        State::Waiting { attempt, .. } => format!(\"Waiting (try {attempt})\"),\n        State::Bound { mapped_port } => format!(\"Bound :{mapped_port}\"),\n        State::Failed => \"Failed\".to_string(),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Drive a full handshake against a synthetic lossy network and return the\n/// final state plus a step-by-step log. Pure: same arguments, same result,\n/// forever. There is no socket and no wall clock anywhere in here.\npub fn run(\n    txid: u32,\n    seed: u64,\n    rto: Millis,\n    max_attempts: u32,\n    loss_percent: u32,\n) -> (State, Vec<LogRow>) {\n    let mut binding = StunBinding::new(txid, 0, rto, max_attempts);\n    let mut net = Lcg(seed);\n    let mut log: Vec<LogRow> = Vec::new();\n    let mut clock: Millis = 0;\n\n    // A safety cap so a pathological config can never spin forever.\n    for _ in 0..64 {\n        // 1. Send everything the machine has queued.\n        let mut response_arrives = false;\n        while let Some(packet) = binding.poll_transmit() {\n            if let Packet::Request { txid } = packet {\n                if net.drops(loss_percent) {\n                    log.push(LogRow {\n                        clock,\n                        kind: EventKind::Drop,\n                        detail: format!(\"request {txid:#010x} lost on the way out\"),\n                        state: state_label(binding.state()),\n                    });\n                } else if net.drops(loss_percent) {\n                    log.push(LogRow {\n                        clock,\n                        kind: EventKind::Drop,\n                        detail: format!(\"request {txid:#010x} arrives; the reply is lost\"),\n                        state: state_label(binding.state()),\n                    });\n                } else {\n                    log.push(LogRow {\n                        clock,\n                        kind: EventKind::Send,\n                        detail: format!(\"request {txid:#010x} reaches the server\"),\n                        state: state_label(binding.state()),\n                    });\n                    response_arrives = true;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        // 2. If a reply survived, hand it back to the machine.\n        if response_arrives {\n            binding.handle_input(\n                Packet::Response {\n                    txid,\n                    mapped_port: 51820,\n                },\n                clock,\n            );\n            log.push(LogRow {\n                clock,\n                kind: EventKind::Recv,\n                detail: \"response accepted; mapped port :51820 learned\".to_string(),\n                state: state_label(binding.state()),\n            });\n        }\n\n        // 3. Are we finished?\n        if matches!(binding.state(), State::Bound { .. } | State::Failed) {\n            break;\n        }\n\n        // 4. Nothing came back: jump the clock to the deadline and tick timers.\n        let Some(deadline) = binding.poll_timeout() else {\n            break;\n        };\n        clock = deadline;\n        binding.handle_timeout(clock);\n        match binding.state() {\n            State::Failed => log.push(LogRow {\n                clock,\n                kind: EventKind::Fail,\n                detail: format!(\"silence after {max_attempts} attempts; giving up\"),\n                state: state_label(binding.state()),\n            }),\n            State::Waiting { attempt, .. } => log.push(LogRow {\n                clock,\n                kind: EventKind::Timer,\n                detail: format!(\"deadline reached; retransmitting (attempt {attempt})\"),\n                state: state_label(binding.state()),\n            }),\n            _ => {}\n        }\n    }\n\n    (binding.state(), log)\n}\n\n// ── Notebook rendering (glue only; NOT part of the protocol) ─────────────────\n//\n// Everything below exists purely to draw the machine for this notebook, using\n// nothing but `String` building. The protocol above never mentions SVG, HTML,\n// or the browser: that is exactly the point.\n\n/// Map a state to `(active_index, accent_color)` for the diagram.\nfn active_node(s: State) -> (usize, &'static str) {\n    match s {\n        State::Idle => (0, \"#e94560\"),\n        State::Waiting { .. } => (1, \"#e94560\"),\n        State::Bound { .. } => (2, \"#3ecf8e\"),\n        State::Failed => (3, \"#ff6b6b\"),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Draw the four-state diagram, lighting up whichever state is active.\npub fn render_diagram(active: State) -> String {\n    let (active_idx, accent) = active_node(active);\n\n    // (index, x, y, w, h, label)\n    let nodes = [\n        (0usize, 40, 95, 120, 52, \"Idle\"),\n        (1, 260, 95, 150, 52, \"Waiting\"),\n        (2, 540, 30, 120, 52, \"Bound\"),\n        (3, 540, 160, 120, 52, \"Failed\"),\n    ];\n\n    let mut svg = String::new();\n    svg.push_str(\n        \"<svg viewBox=\\\"0 0 700 250\\\" xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" \\\n         style=\\\"width:100%;max-width:700px;background:#161625;border-radius:10px;\\\n         font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace\\\">\",\n    );\n    // Arrowhead marker.\n    svg.push_str(\n        \"<defs><marker id=\\\"ah\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 10 10\\\" refX=\\\"9\\\" refY=\\\"5\\\" \\\n         markerWidth=\\\"7\\\" markerHeight=\\\"7\\\" orient=\\\"auto-start-reverse\\\">\\\n         <path d=\\\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z\\\" fill=\\\"#6b6b8a\\\"/></marker></defs>\",\n    );\n\n    // ── Edges ──\n    let edge = |from: &str, to: &str, label: &str, lx: i32, ly: i32| -> String {\n        format!(\n            \"<path d=\\\"M{from} L{to}\\\" stroke=\\\"#4a4a66\\\" stroke-width=\\\"2\\\" fill=\\\"none\\\" \\\n             marker-end=\\\"url(#ah)\\\"/>\\\n             <text x=\\\"{lx}\\\" y=\\\"{ly}\\\" fill=\\\"#8888aa\\\" font-size=\\\"12\\\" \\\n             text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">{label}</text>\"\n        )\n    };\n    // Idle -> Waiting\n    svg.push_str(&edge(\"160,121\", \"252,121\", \"first request\", 210, 112));\n    // Waiting -> Bound\n    svg.push_str(&edge(\"412,108\", \"534,66\", \"response\", 500, 96));\n    // Waiting -> Failed\n    svg.push_str(&edge(\"412,134\", \"534,182\", \"max retries\", 500, 168));\n    // Waiting self-loop (retransmit)\n    svg.push_str(\n        \"<path d=\\\"M300,95 C300,45 370,45 370,95\\\" stroke=\\\"#4a4a66\\\" stroke-width=\\\"2\\\" \\\n         fill=\\\"none\\\" marker-end=\\\"url(#ah)\\\"/>\\\n         <text x=\\\"335\\\" y=\\\"38\\\" fill=\\\"#8888aa\\\" font-size=\\\"12\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">\\\n         timeout / retransmit</text>\",\n    );\n\n    // ── Nodes ──\n    for (idx, x, y, w, h, label) in nodes {\n        let is_active = idx == active_idx;\n        let (fill, stroke, text_color, weight) = if is_active {\n            (accent, accent, \"#ffffff\", \"700\")\n        } else {\n            (\"#232338\", \"#3a3a55\", \"#9a9ab5\", \"500\")\n        };\n        let glow = if is_active {\n            format!(\" filter=\\\"drop-shadow(0 0 6px {accent})\\\"\")\n        } else {\n            String::new()\n        };\n        svg.push_str(&format!(\n            \"<g{glow}><rect x=\\\"{x}\\\" y=\\\"{y}\\\" width=\\\"{w}\\\" height=\\\"{h}\\\" rx=\\\"9\\\" \\\n             fill=\\\"{fill}\\\" stroke=\\\"{stroke}\\\" stroke-width=\\\"2\\\"/>\\\n             <text x=\\\"{tx}\\\" y=\\\"{ty}\\\" fill=\\\"{text_color}\\\" font-size=\\\"16\\\" \\\n             font-weight=\\\"{weight}\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">{label}</text></g>\",\n            tx = x + w / 2,\n            ty = y + h / 2 + 6,\n        ));\n    }\n\n    svg.push_str(&format!(\n        \"<text x=\\\"350\\\" y=\\\"242\\\" fill=\\\"#c7c7de\\\" font-size=\\\"13\\\" text-anchor=\\\"middle\\\">\\\n         current state: {}</text>\",\n        state_label(active)\n    ));\n    svg.push_str(\"</svg>\");\n    svg\n}\n\n/// Render a driven run's log as a styled HTML table with color-coded badges.\npub fn render_log(log: &[LogRow]) -> String {\n    let badge = |kind: EventKind| -> (&'static str, &'static str) {\n        match kind {\n            EventKind::Send => (\"SEND\", \"#4fc3f7\"),\n            EventKind::Recv => (\"RECV\", \"#3ecf8e\"),\n            EventKind::Drop => (\"DROP\", \"#ff6b6b\"),\n            EventKind::Timer => (\"TIMER\", \"#ffd166\"),\n            EventKind::Fail => (\"FAIL\", \"#ff6b6b\"),\n        }\n    };\n\n    let mut html = String::new();\n    html.push_str(\n        \"<table style=\\\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;\\\n         font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:13px;\\\n         background:#161625;color:#e0e0ea;border-radius:8px;overflow:hidden\\\">\",\n    );\n    html.push_str(\n        \"<thead><tr style=\\\"background:#20203a;color:#9a9ab5;text-align:left\\\">\\\n         <th style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">#</th>\\\n         <th style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">clock</th>\\\n         <th style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">event</th>\\\n         <th style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">what happened</th>\\\n         <th style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">state after</th></tr></thead><tbody>\",\n    );\n\n    for (i, row) in log.iter().enumerate() {\n        let (tag, color) = badge(row.kind);\n        html.push_str(&format!(\n            \"<tr style=\\\"border-top:1px solid #2a2a44\\\">\\\n             <td style=\\\"padding:8px 12px;color:#6b6b8a\\\">{n}</td>\\\n             <td style=\\\"padding:8px 12px;color:#c7c7de\\\">{clock} ms</td>\\\n             <td style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">\\\n             <span style=\\\"background:{color}22;color:{color};padding:2px 8px;\\\n             border-radius:4px;font-weight:700\\\">{tag}</span></td>\\\n             <td style=\\\"padding:8px 12px\\\">{detail}</td>\\\n             <td style=\\\"padding:8px 12px;color:#bb9cf0\\\">{state}</td></tr>\",\n            n = i + 1,\n            clock = row.clock,\n            detail = row.detail,\n            state = row.state,\n        ));\n    }\n\n    html.push_str(\"</tbody></table>\");\n    html\n}\n",
  "description": "A STUN-style handshake built as a pure state machine, then run, lost, retransmitted, and replayed deterministically inside a browser wasm cell.",
  "tags": [
    "sans-io",
    "protocol",
    "state-machine",
    "networking",
    "async",
    "determinism"
  ],
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "The trick every Rust networking crate reinvents",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# The trick every Rust networking crate reinvents\n\nLook at the Rust crates that got protocol design *right* and you find the same move underneath all of them: the protocol logic is there, and the I/O has been surgically removed. No sockets. No timers. No `Instant::now()`. Just a state machine you feed bytes and timestamps.\n\nThey call it **sans-IO**, and the family keeps growing. [quinn-proto](https://docs.rs/quinn-proto) is the QUIC state machine with the networking sliced out. [str0m](https://github.com/algesten/str0m) is a fully synchronous WebRTC stack. And as of **January 2026**, [sctp-proto](https://github.com/algesten/sctp-proto) moved under the str0m maintainers, quietly consolidating a whole sans-IO protocol family (WebRTC, SCTP, DTLS, STUN) under one roof.\n\n[Firezone's explainer](https://www.firezone.dev/blog/sans-io) pinned down the shape everyone converged on. Four methods:\n\n- `handle_input(packet, now)`: feed in a datagram you received\n- `handle_timeout(now)`: advance the timers\n- `poll_transmit() -> Option<Transmit>`: pull out a datagram it wants to send\n- `poll_timeout() -> Option<Instant>`: ask when it wants to be woken next\n\nThe load-bearing detail is `now`. Time is always an **injected timestamp**, never sampled from a clock inside the machine. That one discipline is what buys portability, testability, and determinism all at once. It is also why the exact same handshake can run under tokio, inside no_std firmware, and *right here in a browser wasm cell*, which is what the rest of this notebook does.\n\nLet me not overclaim: sans-IO is not free, and I will get to the honest trade-offs at the end. First, let me build one.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "The whole protocol is an enum and a match",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## The whole protocol is an enum and a match\n\nHere is the claim that sounds like a stretch until you see it: a sans-IO handshake is *just* an enum for the state, a `Vec` for the outbound queue, and four methods that pattern-match. No async, no traits, no dependencies. Open the **Shared Source** panel and read the state machine top to bottom: it is about **eighty lines**, and everything below it in the panel is only the rendering glue that draws it for this notebook.\n\nThe toy protocol is a **STUN-style binding request**: a client behind a NAT asks a server what public address it appears to come from, and the server replies with the mapped port. If that reply gets lost, the client retransmits on a timer, backing off each time per [RFC 5389](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5389), and eventually gives up. Four states cover the whole story:\n\n- **Idle**: constructed, nothing sent yet\n- **Waiting**: request in flight, retransmit timer armed\n- **Bound**: the server answered, we learned our mapped port\n- **Failed**: the retransmit budget ran out\n\nEvery transition is driven by one of the four methods, and *not one of them* reaches for a socket or the clock. Watch it happen, first by hand, then in a loop.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Meet the machine",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// A transaction on our own clock, in milliseconds. No socket in sight.\nlet mut binding = shared::StunBinding::new(0x1a2b_3c4d, 0, 500, 7);\n\nlet mut trace: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\ntrace.push(format!(\n    \"new()            -> {}\",\n    shared::state_label(binding.state())\n));\n\n// The machine already queued its first request. Ask for it.\nlet first = binding.poll_transmit();\ntrace.push(format!(\"poll_transmit()  -> {first:?}\"));\ntrace.push(format!(\n    \"poll_timeout()   -> wake me at {:?}\",\n    binding.poll_timeout()\n));\n\n// Pretend it hit the wire, reached the server, and a reply came back at t = 120.\nbinding.handle_input(\n    shared::Packet::Response { txid: 0x1a2b_3c4d, mapped_port: 51820 },\n    120,\n);\ntrace.push(format!(\n    \"handle_input()   -> {}\",\n    shared::state_label(binding.state())\n));\ntrace.push(format!(\"poll_timeout()   -> {:?}\", binding.poll_timeout()));\n\nlet listing = format!(\n    \"<pre style=\\\"background:#161625;color:#e0e0ea;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;\\\n     font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:13px;\\\n     line-height:1.6;margin:0\\\">{}</pre>\",\n    trace.join(\"\\n\"),\n);\n\n(Html(listing), Svg(shared::render_diagram(binding.state())))",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_3",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "You own the event loop",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## You own the event loop\n\nHere is the part sans-IO advocates love and skeptics grumble about: the machine does not run itself. **You** own the loop. You pull datagrams out with `poll_transmit`, you decide what happens to them on the wire, you advance the clock, and you hand replies back with `handle_input`. The machine only ever answers one question: *given what you just told me, what now?*\n\nThat is more work, and I will own that. It is also a superpower, because the loop can be a **synthetic network** with no network in it. The `run` driver in the Shared Source does exactly that:\n\n- a deterministic **LCG** (a four-line pseudo-random generator, no `rand` dependency) flips a weighted coin to drop packets\n- the clock is a plain `u64` of milliseconds that I *jump* straight to the next deadline, so a twelve-second backoff simulates in nanoseconds\n- every step is logged, so the diagrams and tables below are just a rendering of what actually happened\n\nSame seed, same drops, same result, forever. Let me run it three ways: a clean network, a lossy one, and a black hole.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_4",
      "order": 4,
      "label": "A clean run: zero loss",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// A friendly network: zero loss. The machine binds on the very first try.\nlet (final_state, log) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, 1, 500, 7, 0);\n\n(\n    Svg(shared::render_diagram(final_state)),\n    Html(shared::render_log(&log)),\n)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_5",
      "order": 5,
      "label": "A lossy run that recovers",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// 45% loss on every hop, with a generous retransmit budget. The seed is fixed,\n// so the exact sequence of drops below reproduces byte for byte: that is the trick.\nlet (final_state, log) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, 12, 500, 7, 45);\n\n(\n    Svg(shared::render_diagram(final_state)),\n    Html(shared::render_log(&log)),\n)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_6",
      "order": 6,
      "label": "When the network never answers",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// A black hole: 100% loss. Every request vanishes, the timers fire, and the\n// machine walks its budget down to a clean Failed. No panic, no leak, no hang.\nlet (final_state, log) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, 1, 400, 5, 100);\n\n(\n    Svg(shared::render_diagram(final_state)),\n    Html(shared::render_log(&log)),\n)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_7",
      "order": 7,
      "label": "Determinism means replay",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// Determinism is testable, not a slogan. Run the same lossy scenario twice and\n// diff the logs. A protocol that read the clock or the socket could never\n// promise this.\nlet (state_a, log_a) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, 12, 500, 7, 45);\nlet (state_b, log_b) = shared::run(0x1a2b_3c4d, 12, 500, 7, 45);\n\nlet identical = state_a == state_b\n    && log_a.len() == log_b.len()\n    && log_a.iter().zip(&log_b).all(|(x, y)| {\n        x.clock == y.clock && x.kind == y.kind && x.detail == y.detail && x.state == y.state\n    });\n\nlet verdict = format!(\n    \"<div style=\\\"background:#161625;color:#e0e0ea;padding:18px;border-radius:8px;\\\n     font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:14px;line-height:1.8\\\">\\\n     two independent runs, one seed<br>\\\n     steps recorded: <b style=\\\"color:#4fc3f7\\\">{}</b> and <b style=\\\"color:#4fc3f7\\\">{}</b><br>\\\n     final state: <b style=\\\"color:#bb9cf0\\\">{}</b> and <b style=\\\"color:#bb9cf0\\\">{}</b><br>\\\n     logs identical: <b style=\\\"color:{}\\\">{}</b></div>\",\n    log_a.len(),\n    log_b.len(),\n    shared::state_label(state_a),\n    shared::state_label(state_b),\n    if identical { \"#3ecf8e\" } else { \"#ff6b6b\" },\n    identical,\n);\n\nHtml(verdict)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_8",
      "order": 8,
      "label": "The same sixty lines, everywhere",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## The same eighty lines, everywhere\n\nNothing in the Shared Source knows it is running in a browser. That is the entire return on the discipline, and it shows up in three concrete places.\n\n**It is portable.** The handshake has no runtime and no I/O, so it drops into a tokio task, a no_std embedded loop, or a wasm cell without changing a line. You just saw the third case: that state machine compiled to `wasm32-unknown-unknown` and ran in a Web Worker to draw these diagrams. It never noticed.\n\n**It is testable without a network.** Scroll back to the lossy run. I simulated 45% packet loss, watched the retransmit timers fire, and confirmed recovery, with *zero* sockets and no mocking framework anywhere. The \"network\" was a coin flip. Testing the failure path of a real socket stack is a genuine pain; here it is a function argument.\n\n**It is deterministic, which means bugs replay.** The determinism cell ran the same seed twice and diffed the logs byte for byte. When a fuzzer finds the one packet sequence that wedges your protocol, it hands you a seed, and that seed reproduces the exact failure on your laptop every single time. A protocol that reads the clock or the socket directly can never promise that.\n\nNow the honest part, because sans-IO is not a free lunch:\n\n- **You write the event loop, and the glue is real.** Every consumer re-implements the poll-transmit, arm-the-timer, feed-the-input dance. It is boilerplate, and it is easy to get subtly wrong.\n- **The ergonomics can feel inside-out.** An `async fn` reads top to bottom; a hand-cranked state machine does not. For a simple request and response, plenty of people call this overkill, and the discourse about *when* it earns its keep is very much alive.\n- **You give up the free state that `.await` keeps for you.** Everything the machine must remember becomes an explicit field. That is exactly why it is inspectable, and also exactly why there is more to type.\n\nMy take: for anything with retransmits, congestion control, or a spec you have to match byte for byte, the determinism and testability win by a mile. For a one-shot request, open a socket and go home.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_9",
      "order": 9,
      "label": "Who is carrying this",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Who is carrying this\n\nSans-IO is not a fringe experiment; it is how a growing slice of the Rust networking ecosystem is actually built, and the roster is worth knowing.\n\n- **[str0m](https://github.com/algesten/str0m)** (Martin Algesten): a fully synchronous, sans-IO WebRTC stack, and since January 2026 the new home of **[sctp-proto](https://github.com/algesten/sctp-proto)** as well.\n- **[quinn-proto](https://docs.rs/quinn-proto)**: the QUIC state machine underneath Quinn, and the pattern's most-cited proof that it scales all the way up to a real, complicated protocol.\n- **[Firezone](https://www.firezone.dev/blog/sans-io)**: the team whose explainer handed everyone the shared vocabulary, four methods and an injected clock, that this notebook borrows wholesale.\n\nThe pattern is *old* (it is just a state machine, the thing we have been teaching in school forever) and *newly fashionable*, because Rust's `async` story made the cost of **not** separating I/O from logic impossible to ignore. The punchline is the one you just watched run: a protocol that never touches a socket will run anywhere you can call four functions, a notebook cell included.\n\nGo open the Shared Source and change the loss rate. The state machine underneath is about eighty lines, and every run replays exactly. Break it, replay it, fix it.",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ],
  "expand_code": true
}